1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,075
Capability compares two distributions. One is

2
00:00:04,175 --> 00:00:07,508
the customer's. One is the process's.

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:11,532
One dimension, one axis, millimetres.

4
00:00:11,616 --> 00:00:14,641
The customer speaks in limits. Anything

5
00:00:14,741 --> 00:00:17,204
between these lines is accepted.

6
00:00:17,300 --> 00:00:22,240
The process answers with its own spread. It never read the drawing.

7
00:00:22,333 --> 00:00:26,453
Tolerance is zero point six millimetres wide. Six

8
00:00:26,553 --> 00:00:29,985
sigma of process is zero point four five.

9
00:00:30,083 --> 00:00:33,898
Cp is those two widths divided. One point

10
00:00:33,998 --> 00:00:38,863
three three. Pure geometry, assuming a centred mean.

11
00:00:38,950 --> 00:00:42,930
Now drift the mean. The spread does not change.

12
00:00:43,016 --> 00:00:48,196
One gap shrinks. The other opens. Cp cannot tell.

13
00:00:48,283 --> 00:00:52,335
The near side leaks. That is where parts fail.

14
00:00:52,433 --> 00:00:56,801
Cpk takes the smaller one sided ratio. Zero

15
00:00:56,901 --> 00:01:00,853
point eight nine. The near gap decides.

16
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:06,562
Every Cpk value is a promise about defect rate.

17
00:01:06,650 --> 00:01:11,474
Each row: a Cpk, the parts per million expected outside

18
00:01:11,574 --> 00:01:15,862
the limit, and the same number in plain counting.

19
00:01:15,950 --> 00:01:19,515
These are near tail figures, computed at render time

20
00:01:19,615 --> 00:01:22,546
by the same function the test suite checks.

21
00:01:22,633 --> 00:01:25,293
Nothing is read off a table. Change

22
00:01:25,393 --> 00:01:28,053
the formula and this slide changes.

